{{also|impénétrable}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{PIE root|en|peh₂}}
From {{etyl|frm|en}} {{m|frm|impenetrable}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|impenetrabilis}}.

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# Not [[penetrable]].
#: ''The fortress is '''impenetrable''', so it cannot be taken.''
#* {{quote-journal|date=2012|author=John Branch|title=Snow Fall : The Avalanche at Tunnel Creek|newspaper=New York Time|url=http://www.nytimes.com/projects/2012/snow-fall/?smid=fb-nytimes&WT.z_sma=SP_SNT_20130415#/?part=tunnel-creek}}
#*: The avalanche spread and stopped, locking everything it carried into an icy cocoon. It was now a jagged, virtually '''impenetrable''' pile of ice, longer than a football field and nearly as wide.
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} [[incomprehensible#English|Incomprehensible]]; [[inscrutable]].
#: ''Business jargon makes this document '''impenetrable''', I can't understand it.''

====Antonyms====
* {{l|en|penetrable}}

